<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taiwan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taiwan is a large island off the south-eastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> established a colony there in the seventeenth century but lost it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koxinga">Koxinga</a>'s forces in 1662, and the Qing took the island from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-family">Zheng family</a> in 1683. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> of 1894–1895 it became a Japanese colony, supplying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taiwan.jpg" alt="Taiwan" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sun Yat-sen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_China.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The main island of Taiwan, formerly known as Formosa, lies between the East and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Seas</a> in the northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>, some 180 kilometres across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan-strait">Taiwan Strait</a> from the southeastern coast of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It covers 35,808 square kilometres, which is 99 percent of the land the Republic of China controls. Mountain ranges dominate the eastern two-thirds of the island and plains the western third, where most of the population lives. The Penghu Islands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinmen">Kinmen</a>, Matsu and Wuqiu belong to the same jurisdiction. Taiwan lies on the Tropic of Cancer, and typhoons are most common from July to September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Taiwan has been settled for at least 25,000 years. The island was joined to the Asian mainland until sea levels rose about 10,000 years ago, and human remains dated to 20,000 to 30,000 years ago have been found. The ancestors of the indigenous peoples settled it around 6,000 years ago. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> fishermen inhabited the Penghu Islands by 1171, Wang Dayuan gave the first written account of a visit to Taiwan in 1349, and by the 1590s a small number of Chinese from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> were cultivating land in the southwest.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> ceded the island to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, pro-Qing officials proclaimed the Republic of Formosa on 25 May 1895. Japanese forces entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tainan">Tainan</a> and ended this resistance on 21 October 1895. About 6,000 inhabitants died in the initial fighting and some 14,000 in the first year of Japanese rule. Later rebellions, among them the Beipu uprising of 1907 and the Musha incident of 1930, failed. On 25 October 1945 Japan surrendered Taiwan to the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, whose government moved to the island in 1949. Martial law ended in 1987, and presidents have been elected since 1996.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taipei">Taipei</a> is the capital, and more than 23 million people live in the territory under the government of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Mandarin is the primary language of business and education, and the country has no legally designated official language. About 95 percent of the population is ethnically <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> and 2.5 percent belongs to the indigenous Austronesian peoples. The 1947 constitution divides the government into five branches, called Yuan. The political and legal status of Taiwan is contested: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> claims the island as its territory, while the Republic of China has its own armed forces, constitution and institutions, and has not been the representative of China at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> since 1971.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taiwan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Champa grew out of the Sa Huỳnh culture, an Austronesian seafaring people settled on the coast of present-day central Vietnam between about 1000 BCE and 200 CE, who traded across the South China Sea with the Philippine archipelago and <b>Taiwan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch overseas expansion faltered after the loss of <b>Taiwan</b> to Koxinga's forces in 1662 and of New Netherland and New Amsterdam to England in 1664, and the Fourth Anglo-Dutch War of 1780–1784 left both the VOC and the GWC mortally wounded.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The colonies were assigned ranked roles in the empire's economy: <b>Taiwan</b> supplied sugar and rice; Korea provided rice, labour, and later industrial output; Manchukuo after 1932 supplied coal, iron, and strategic minerals essential for military production.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chinese People’s Political Consultative Conference adopted a Common Program in September, and on 1 October 1949 Mao proclaimed the People’s Republic from the Gate of Heavenly Peace in Beijing, which again became the capital, while the Nationalist government withdrew to <b>Taiwan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…oasis cities of the Tarim Basin in Eastern Turkestan, campaigns in Burma, Vietnam and Nepal, the suppression of rebellion on <b>Taiwan</b> and the two Jinchuan wars in Sichuan, and they extended the empire to its greatest extent of some 13 to 14.7 million square kilometres by various estimates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>On <b>Taiwan</b> after 1949, land reform culminating in the Land-to-the-Tiller programme of 1953, American aid and export-led manufacturing produced rapid growth, and by the 1990s the island was a leading producer of electronics and semiconductors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
